      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To observe the effects of κ-opioid receptor agonist U50, 488H on myocardial ischemia and reperfusion injury and related mechanism."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Rats were randomly divided into sham operation, myocardial ischemia and reperfusion(I/R, 30 min ischemia followed by 120 min reperfusion), and MI/R+U50, 488H (1.5 mg/kg) and I/R+U50, 488H+ selective κ-opioid receptor antagonist Nor-BNI (2 mg/kg, n = 8 each). The infarction size and the incidence of ventricular arrhythmias were observed.Real-time PCR and DAB staining were used to define the myocardium Toll-like receptor 4(TLR4) expression. Myeloperoxidase level, TNF-α induction and the expression of NF-κB were also examined in rats."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "After I/R, the expressions of myocardial TLR4 and NF-κB increased significantly both in ischemia area and area at risk. Compared with I/R, κ-opioid receptor stimulation with U50, 488H significantly attenuated the expressions of TLR4 and NF-κB and reduced myeloperoxidase (MPO) levels, myocardial TNF-α production, myocardial infarct sizes and the incidence of ventricular arrhythmias and arrhythmia score (2.9 ± 0.7 vs. 4.4 ± 0.9, P < 0.05) , above effects of U50, 488H were partly abolished by co-treatment with Nor-BNI."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "These data provide evidence for the first time that κ-opioid receptor stimulation could attenuate myocardial I/R injury via downregulating TLR4/NF-κB signaling in rats."
        }
      ]},
